PROBLEM:
When an application needs to create a very large number of objects with similar or identical
data, each object stores duplicate information. This leads to excessive memory usage, poor performance,
and increased object creation time.

Classic Example:
In a 3D game rendering a forest with 10,000 trees, if each tree object individually stores its own heavy
3D mesh and texture data, the app will run out of memory instantly.

SOLUTION:
-The Flyweight Pattern separates data into:
    Intrinsic State (Shared): Common data that doesn't change (e.g., tree image, font, texture).
    Extrinsic State (Unique): Data that changes for each object (e.g., position, size, health).

